WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:07.100
&gt;&gt; So, it's my pleasure
to welcome Mark Briers to

00:00:07.101 --> 00:00:09.550
MSR to give a talk
today entitled Turing,

00:00:09.551 --> 00:00:10.925
Bayes and Cyber Security.

00:00:10.926 --> 00:00:12.230
Mark is the Program Director of

00:00:12.231 --> 00:00:14.115
Security the Alan
Turing Institute.

00:00:14.116 --> 00:00:16.080
Prior to that he
worked for 16 years in

00:00:16.081 --> 00:00:17.430
the Defense Industry primarily in

00:00:17.431 --> 00:00:19.725
the areas of statistical
data analysis.

00:00:19.726 --> 00:00:21.240
His research interests include

00:00:21.241 --> 00:00:22.565
scalable bayesian inference,

00:00:22.566 --> 00:00:25.070
sequential inference,
and anomaly detection,

00:00:25.071 --> 00:00:26.785
particularly areas
of cyber security.

00:00:26.786 --> 00:00:29.120
So thank you for doing
this talk today, Mark.

00:00:29.121 --> 00:00:31.190
&gt;&gt; No, thank you. Thank you
for inviting me and thank you

00:00:31.191 --> 00:00:34.650
for coming here on
early- is it Wednesday?

00:00:34.651 --> 00:00:36.225
I've kind of- Wednesday
or Thursday.

00:00:36.226 --> 00:00:38.460
I've lost count what day it is.

00:00:38.461 --> 00:00:39.540
Jet lag and what gave you .

00:00:39.541 --> 00:00:42.525
So thank you for
hosting me today.

00:00:42.526 --> 00:00:44.390
So, what I want to do is

00:00:44.391 --> 00:00:46.340
to split this talk
essentially into

00:00:46.341 --> 00:00:48.770
three parts I think I
changed talk title as

00:00:48.771 --> 00:00:51.800
well just to keep
us all on our toes.

00:00:51.801 --> 00:00:54.560
So, what I want to
do is to discuss

00:00:54.561 --> 00:00:57.720
the Alan Turing Institute
and introduce that to you.

00:00:57.721 --> 00:01:00.990
Your organization and
hopefully motivate you,

00:01:00.991 --> 00:01:03.140
inspire you and convince you that

00:01:03.141 --> 00:01:05.540
it's a worthwhile opportunity
to collaborate with us,

00:01:05.541 --> 00:01:07.420
if that's of interest
to you guys.

00:01:07.421 --> 00:01:10.940
Then kind of in some
sense the segue from

00:01:10.941 --> 00:01:15.470
Turing and the Turing Institutes
and Alan Turing the man

00:01:15.471 --> 00:01:18.440
into the use of Bayesian
statistics in the context of

00:01:18.441 --> 00:01:20.150
security related applications and

00:01:20.151 --> 00:01:22.840
Turing's work at Bletchley Park.

00:01:22.841 --> 00:01:24.670
So there's a couple
of slides on and

00:01:24.671 --> 00:01:28.205
some relatively recent
publications of

00:01:28.206 --> 00:01:30.410
Alan Turing's that've
been declassified

00:01:30.411 --> 00:01:33.110
and pushed out that not
many people are aware of,

00:01:33.111 --> 00:01:35.330
which demonstrate Turing's use

00:01:35.331 --> 00:01:37.705
of Bayes which is quite
cool in my opinion.

00:01:37.706 --> 00:01:42.290
Then finally, try
to kind of- there's

00:01:42.291 --> 00:01:44.075
so many tenuous links
in this talk

00:01:44.076 --> 00:01:46.520
and finally link the kind

00:01:46.521 --> 00:01:48.590
of Bayesian story to my work in

00:01:48.591 --> 00:01:51.020
cybersecurity and give you
an overview of what I'm

00:01:51.021 --> 00:01:52.920
trying to achieve
with the work at

00:01:52.921 --> 00:01:57.355
the institutes in the context
of cybersecurity.

00:01:57.356 --> 00:02:03.800
My background is I'm
a statistician by kind of PhD.

00:02:03.801 --> 00:02:06.050
Specifically sequential
Monte-Carlo that was

00:02:06.051 --> 00:02:08.880
the subject of my PhD thesis.

00:02:08.881 --> 00:02:12.589
So, you'll see lots of use
of those kinds of algorithms

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:14.375
and I've been taught

00:02:14.376 --> 00:02:17.540
Bayesian statistics from
an undergraduate level.

00:02:17.541 --> 00:02:19.490
So, all I know is
base so if there's

00:02:19.491 --> 00:02:22.550
a question my solution
is a Bayesian solution,

00:02:22.551 --> 00:02:25.630
so I will make
a slight apology for being

00:02:25.631 --> 00:02:30.200
completely kind of shortsighted
in my approach to things.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:33.380
So, the Alan Turing Institute,

00:02:33.381 --> 00:02:35.210
I've got a few slides on
the Alan Turing institute.

00:02:35.211 --> 00:02:38.605
So, we are a charity in the UK,

00:02:38.606 --> 00:02:41.620
and we were set up by
the UK government,

00:02:41.621 --> 00:02:44.685
well, formally announced
in March 2014.

00:02:44.686 --> 00:02:47.330
And what was happening in
the UK and perhaps around

00:02:47.331 --> 00:02:48.740
the world but specifically in

00:02:48.741 --> 00:02:50.545
the context of the UK landscape,

00:02:50.546 --> 00:02:53.635
there's lots of
work being done in,

00:02:53.636 --> 00:02:55.700
well, I guess we know
call it data science,

00:02:55.701 --> 00:02:58.150
perhaps we'd even call it
artificial intelligence given

00:02:58.151 --> 00:03:00.280
the evolution of
the type that gets

00:03:00.281 --> 00:03:03.420
such these data
related activities.

00:03:03.421 --> 00:03:05.930
But back in the day it was
referred to as big data.

00:03:05.931 --> 00:03:08.570
So, there are lots of
difference initiatives,

00:03:08.571 --> 00:03:11.870
research initiatives or
application relation initiatives

00:03:11.871 --> 00:03:14.850
happening in academia
and in industry.

00:03:14.851 --> 00:03:17.520
There's lots of fragmentation
even within the UK.

00:03:17.521 --> 00:03:20.990
So, the UK government's was
particularly keen to ensure

00:03:20.991 --> 00:03:23.600
that UK society and

00:03:23.601 --> 00:03:26.360
UK PLC can benefit
from these activities,

00:03:26.361 --> 00:03:29.630
and so they set up
a National Institutes for data

00:03:29.631 --> 00:03:32.720
science and artificial
intelligence related research

00:03:32.721 --> 00:03:35.990
to provide national level
leadership and ensure

00:03:35.991 --> 00:03:39.260
that UK society and
in collaboration with

00:03:39.261 --> 00:03:42.200
our international partners
internationally we can

00:03:42.201 --> 00:03:43.775
benefit from research activities

00:03:43.776 --> 00:03:45.815
taking place in these areas.

00:03:45.816 --> 00:03:47.990
So, the institutes-
and you can think

00:03:47.991 --> 00:03:50.860
of as sitting at the center
of two networks.

00:03:50.861 --> 00:03:52.820
On one hand we've
got a network of

00:03:52.821 --> 00:03:54.590
academic related institutions and

00:03:54.591 --> 00:03:56.210
I'll talk about that in
more detail shortly,

00:03:56.211 --> 00:03:58.220
on the other side
we have a number

00:03:58.221 --> 00:04:02.120
of industry or government
related partners through

00:04:02.121 --> 00:04:04.955
which we try to kind of get
real-world problems and

00:04:04.956 --> 00:04:08.395
ensure that our research has
real-world applicability.

00:04:08.396 --> 00:04:10.980
Our job is essentially to try to

00:04:10.981 --> 00:04:12.650
make some sense out of

00:04:12.651 --> 00:04:14.605
all the stuff that's going
on in academia and industry,

00:04:14.606 --> 00:04:16.310
connect things up and

00:04:16.311 --> 00:04:18.915
provide strategic leadership
at the national level.

00:04:18.916 --> 00:04:21.410
Alongside that were
also expected to

00:04:21.411 --> 00:04:23.810
train and educate everybody

00:04:23.811 --> 00:04:29.030
from some of my relatives
who know nothing

00:04:29.031 --> 00:04:31.520
about data science all
the way through to

00:04:31.521 --> 00:04:32.990
professors who want to know more

00:04:32.991 --> 00:04:34.680
about a specific
specialized area.

00:04:34.681 --> 00:04:35.950
So we're expected to kind of

00:04:35.951 --> 00:04:37.620
provide training and
education as well.

00:04:37.621 --> 00:04:39.630
So we've got quite agreement

00:04:39.631 --> 00:04:43.205
and we get a
reasonable amounts of

00:04:43.206 --> 00:04:46.310
funding from central government

00:04:46.311 --> 00:04:47.900
which is actually
funneled through

00:04:47.901 --> 00:04:49.725
one of our research council's,

00:04:49.726 --> 00:04:52.430
similar to the NSF I guess.

00:04:52.431 --> 00:04:54.230
So we're considered to be

00:04:54.231 --> 00:04:55.895
a strategic government
investment.

00:04:55.896 --> 00:05:01.180
That means that we're

00:05:01.181 --> 00:05:02.725
not considered to be

00:05:02.726 --> 00:05:04.880
predatory in any sense
because we are a charity,

00:05:04.881 --> 00:05:06.705
we've got some
charitable goals around

00:05:06.706 --> 00:05:09.250
social good and
training and education,

00:05:09.251 --> 00:05:10.350
people don't see us as a threat,

00:05:10.351 --> 00:05:11.900
a commercial threat or otherwise.

00:05:11.901 --> 00:05:13.570
They see us as a
way in which they

00:05:13.571 --> 00:05:16.240
can utilize our expertise
and the expertise

00:05:16.241 --> 00:05:20.280
that we bring in to benefit
their organizations.

00:05:20.281 --> 00:05:21.570
And in doing so we can benefit

00:05:21.571 --> 00:05:25.350
or we can align ourselves
to our charitable goals.

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:28.510
So, we've got a network of

00:05:28.511 --> 00:05:29.980
university partners and I'll

00:05:29.981 --> 00:05:31.420
describe how that
works in a moment,

00:05:31.421 --> 00:05:33.370
but these are the
university partners that we

00:05:33.371 --> 00:05:35.350
have at the moment so
we started off with

00:05:35.351 --> 00:05:36.850
five universities when we were

00:05:36.851 --> 00:05:39.100
initially created so
we were announced by

00:05:39.101 --> 00:05:41.690
George Osborne who was our
and it was our Chancellor of

00:05:41.691 --> 00:05:44.310
the Exchequer back in 2014.

00:05:44.311 --> 00:05:47.950
We kind of started in 2016
really doing any activity.

00:05:47.951 --> 00:05:50.885
So October 2016, so
about two years old now.

00:05:50.886 --> 00:05:52.790
We started with
five universities.

00:05:52.791 --> 00:05:55.355
So, we had a competition
in the UK to decide

00:05:55.356 --> 00:05:59.150
the five best universities
in data science and AI and

00:05:59.151 --> 00:06:00.410
the top five one and there'll be

00:06:00.411 --> 00:06:02.900
no surprises there in
terms of the names.

00:06:02.901 --> 00:06:05.235
These are household names
in some respects.

00:06:05.236 --> 00:06:07.700
Then in the past
six months we've increased

00:06:07.701 --> 00:06:09.830
our network with
eight more universities.

00:06:09.831 --> 00:06:12.890
We're now up to 13
universities, if I can count,

00:06:12.891 --> 00:06:15.785
and what we do is we

00:06:15.786 --> 00:06:19.100
essentially second
academics from universities

00:06:19.101 --> 00:06:20.420
to work with us and

00:06:20.421 --> 00:06:22.340
provide us with the
intellectual capital that we

00:06:22.341 --> 00:06:24.400
need to be able to

00:06:24.401 --> 00:06:25.780
undertake the research activities

00:06:25.781 --> 00:06:27.490
that we wish to undertake.

00:06:27.491 --> 00:06:31.310
I'll touch on that
like say in a moment.

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:37.020
Just to show you that
we're not London-centric.

00:06:37.021 --> 00:06:40.050
So, the cool thing is that,
well, I think it's cool.

00:06:40.051 --> 00:06:41.275
The cool thing is that

00:06:41.276 --> 00:06:43.510
the Alan Turing Institute's
headquarters is

00:06:43.511 --> 00:06:45.760
based in the British
Library in London.

00:06:45.761 --> 00:06:48.955
So, if you ever get
across the London them do

00:06:48.956 --> 00:06:50.800
please drop me
an email and my email

00:06:50.801 --> 00:06:53.090
address is on the final slide.

00:06:53.091 --> 00:06:56.170
Come visit us, because
the British library has

00:06:56.171 --> 00:06:57.940
some cool artifacts and

00:06:57.941 --> 00:06:59.845
some Mark McCarthy's
hosted there.

00:06:59.846 --> 00:07:01.540
We actually have
an iPad coffee machine

00:07:01.541 --> 00:07:02.650
which is another cool thing.

00:07:02.651 --> 00:07:04.380
Maybe is not so cool.

00:07:04.381 --> 00:07:07.560
In Microsoft you may
have those things.

00:07:07.561 --> 00:07:09.180
But in the UK that's
kind of unique

00:07:09.181 --> 00:07:11.510
so it's one of
the attracting things.

00:07:11.511 --> 00:07:13.665
So Magna Carta and
iPad coffee machine,

00:07:13.666 --> 00:07:15.020
if they can't attract you over

00:07:15.021 --> 00:07:16.870
to the UK then I
don't know what will.

00:07:16.871 --> 00:07:20.840
So, we're based in
London and we have

00:07:20.841 --> 00:07:22.460
two university partners
also based in

00:07:22.461 --> 00:07:24.960
London UCL and Queen Mary.

00:07:24.961 --> 00:07:29.020
Then we have them universities
based around the country.

00:07:29.021 --> 00:07:31.280
So, we have offices
in each one of

00:07:31.281 --> 00:07:33.855
these locations and
I suspect we'll

00:07:33.856 --> 00:07:37.520
have representation in
Northern Ireland and in Wales in

00:07:37.521 --> 00:07:40.790
relatively short timescales
as well or I hope that

00:07:40.791 --> 00:07:42.350
from a personal perspective
so that we have

00:07:42.351 --> 00:07:46.500
full geographic coverage
across the UK.

00:07:46.501 --> 00:07:51.270
So, that's the
academic side in terms

00:07:51.271 --> 00:07:54.020
of the partners that we have.

00:07:54.021 --> 00:07:56.000
So, when we first started, again,

00:07:56.001 --> 00:07:59.555
we had four partners initially,

00:07:59.556 --> 00:08:01.310
so these were launchde these with

00:08:01.311 --> 00:08:03.330
the types of organizations
that we're working with.

00:08:03.331 --> 00:08:05.425
So, Lloyd's Register Foundation.

00:08:05.426 --> 00:08:07.820
They are a charity
themselves and they

00:08:07.821 --> 00:08:10.930
own Lloyd's Register which
is an insurance company.

00:08:10.931 --> 00:08:12.830
That's a commercial entity

00:08:12.831 --> 00:08:14.320
but the profits from
the commercial entity,

00:08:14.321 --> 00:08:16.115
as I understand, they
feedback into the charity.

00:08:16.116 --> 00:08:18.030
The charity's mission
is to essentially make

00:08:18.031 --> 00:08:20.795
the world a safer place
in which to live.

00:08:20.796 --> 00:08:22.490
I guess that benefits the

00:08:22.491 --> 00:08:26.140
insurance arm of
the organization.

00:08:26.141 --> 00:08:29.720
So, Lloyd's Register
particularly interested in,

00:08:29.721 --> 00:08:31.590
they call it data-centric
engineering.

00:08:31.591 --> 00:08:32.740
So, engineering of

00:08:32.741 --> 00:08:34.990
critical national
infrastructure to ensure

00:08:34.991 --> 00:08:36.600
the safety and integrity of

00:08:36.601 --> 00:08:39.055
those infrastructure,
maximize that.

00:08:39.056 --> 00:08:41.545
Intel, marine tested and

00:08:41.546 --> 00:08:45.280
co-designing chipsets
and new algorithms.

00:08:45.281 --> 00:08:48.380
I guess the generating
optimized the sales.

00:08:48.381 --> 00:08:50.905
I lead the interaction

00:08:50.906 --> 00:08:53.140
primarily with
these organizations,

00:08:53.141 --> 00:08:56.020
so GCHQ which is the UK
equivalent of NSA,

00:08:56.021 --> 00:08:57.815
our Ministry of Defense and

00:08:57.816 --> 00:08:59.370
the Ministry Defense
research arm which

00:08:59.371 --> 00:09:03.570
is Defense Science
Technology Lab, DSTL.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:07.165
Then finally, one of
our partners was HSBC.

00:09:07.166 --> 00:09:11.325
I've been told many stories
about HSBC, but allegedly,

00:09:11.326 --> 00:09:13.760
HSBC has 20% of

00:09:13.761 --> 00:09:15.360
the world's financial transaction

00:09:15.361 --> 00:09:17.525
trade flow data going
through the books,

00:09:17.526 --> 00:09:19.980
so that's quite a lot
of data and they want

00:09:19.981 --> 00:09:23.435
to do lots of things
with that data.

00:09:23.436 --> 00:09:28.910
So, there's a co-partnership
happening with HSBC.

00:09:28.911 --> 00:09:30.430
I will go through all of these

00:09:30.431 --> 00:09:31.960
because I realize
it's a little boring.

00:09:31.961 --> 00:09:35.430
The partnership with Microsoft
is one way of presence,

00:09:35.431 --> 00:09:39.320
so we're very grateful to
Microsoft for providing us with

00:09:39.321 --> 00:09:40.960
a million pounds
worth visual credits

00:09:40.961 --> 00:09:43.230
here to do cool stuff,

00:09:43.231 --> 00:09:44.980
but that's where the relationship

00:09:44.981 --> 00:09:46.330
gives a million pounds worth of

00:09:46.331 --> 00:09:50.020
credits and lots more.

00:09:50.021 --> 00:09:51.830
So, part of my motivation for

00:09:51.831 --> 00:09:54.215
being here is to try
to reach out beyond

00:09:54.216 --> 00:09:57.050
those usual credits and

00:09:57.051 --> 00:10:00.545
make some meaningful attempt
at collaboration.

00:10:00.546 --> 00:10:03.780
Some of you may know
Andrew Blake who was

00:10:03.781 --> 00:10:05.280
the director of Microsoft

00:10:05.281 --> 00:10:07.115
Research Cambridge
as I understand it.

00:10:07.116 --> 00:10:08.855
A few years ago he was

00:10:08.856 --> 00:10:11.965
our initial director
at the Institute.

00:10:11.966 --> 00:10:13.950
Andrew is now moved on to

00:10:13.951 --> 00:10:15.980
bigger and better
things I'm told.

00:10:15.981 --> 00:10:19.410
So, there is a link
between MSR and MSL

00:10:19.411 --> 00:10:22.860
Cambridge at least on
the Institutes in London,

00:10:22.861 --> 00:10:25.100
but like I say, I'm really keen

00:10:25.101 --> 00:10:28.260
to develop those relationships.

00:10:28.261 --> 00:10:29.440
So, as I go through
this presentation,

00:10:29.441 --> 00:10:32.475
if anything takes your fancy
then please do contact me.

00:10:32.476 --> 00:10:35.100
I can arrange to link you up with

00:10:35.101 --> 00:10:38.505
the specific academics
or a more general level.

00:10:38.506 --> 00:10:41.595
So, in terms of how
we're structured,

00:10:41.596 --> 00:10:43.180
how many people we hav,

00:10:43.181 --> 00:10:46.765
we're not at the MSL size sadly,

00:10:46.766 --> 00:10:48.480
but we do have
a reasonable amounts

00:10:48.481 --> 00:10:51.235
of people at least
by UK standards.

00:10:51.236 --> 00:10:58.865
So, we have 250 or over
250 Turing Fellows.

00:10:58.866 --> 00:11:00.280
So, we have to use

00:11:00.281 --> 00:11:01.630
the word Turing in
front of everything.

00:11:01.631 --> 00:11:05.030
That's the brand that
we attach ourselves to.

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:08.290
So, what that mean,

00:11:08.291 --> 00:11:10.620
that means that we
succumbed in academics

00:11:10.621 --> 00:11:13.250
from our 13 partner universities.

00:11:13.251 --> 00:11:15.670
So this would be professors
all the way through to

00:11:15.671 --> 00:11:18.350
junior academics working
with us for one, two,

00:11:18.351 --> 00:11:20.500
three days a week on
specific research projects

00:11:20.501 --> 00:11:22.275
or doing simple teaching with us

00:11:22.276 --> 00:11:26.070
or appearing on House of

00:11:26.071 --> 00:11:28.610
Lords committees or trying to

00:11:28.611 --> 00:11:31.430
shape public policy and do
lots of different tactics.

00:11:31.431 --> 00:11:32.620
We actually get them
to do quite a lot

00:11:32.621 --> 00:11:34.195
of work for us in lots
of different ways,

00:11:34.196 --> 00:11:35.690
and it's amazing
academic community.

00:11:35.691 --> 00:11:38.420
So, from the 13 best
UK universities

00:11:38.421 --> 00:11:40.560
we then selected 250,

00:11:40.561 --> 00:11:43.480
so best academics in
this area to work with us.

00:11:43.481 --> 00:11:45.430
So, we have a really
great people of

00:11:45.431 --> 00:11:47.840
academics working with us
and some of the names,

00:11:47.841 --> 00:11:49.270
I won't list them because

00:11:49.271 --> 00:11:50.750
I don't want to show
you demonstrate

00:11:50.751 --> 00:11:52.440
my favorite academics but some of

