00:17:57.626 --> 00:17:59.910
The national economy dashboard.

00:17:59.911 --> 00:18:02.670
So, this is where we're
utilizing the HSBC data,

00:18:02.671 --> 00:18:07.480
so this is, the UK government,

00:18:07.481 --> 00:18:09.510
I am told, has a good
understanding of

00:18:09.511 --> 00:18:12.645
how much trade flows say
between the UK and the US,

00:18:12.646 --> 00:18:15.610
because we know how much
stuff goes over our borders.

00:18:15.611 --> 00:18:16.840
But actually they don't know

00:18:16.841 --> 00:18:18.940
how much trade flows
between London and

00:18:18.941 --> 00:18:21.495
Manchester because this is

00:18:21.496 --> 00:18:22.850
not an easy way of
measuring that.

00:18:22.851 --> 00:18:25.685
So, the HSBC data
it gives us a proxy

00:18:25.686 --> 00:18:27.590
to measuring and
trade flows between

00:18:27.591 --> 00:18:29.505
different geographical
areas in the UK,

00:18:29.506 --> 00:18:32.610
and that allows us
to essentially to

00:18:32.611 --> 00:18:36.300
produce local GDP figures almost,

00:18:36.301 --> 00:18:39.070
and optimized national
level economic strategies

00:18:39.071 --> 00:18:40.385
associated with,

00:18:40.386 --> 00:18:44.790
and the different flows
of trade for instance,

00:18:44.791 --> 00:18:47.325
between different major
cities in the UK.

00:18:47.326 --> 00:18:51.960
That's given the finance parts of

00:18:51.961 --> 00:18:53.440
the governments an ability

00:18:53.441 --> 00:18:56.650
to optimize their interventions,

00:18:56.651 --> 00:18:58.910
and I'm sure it was
a political dimension to that,

00:18:58.911 --> 00:19:00.060
but I would hope that they make

00:19:00.061 --> 00:19:01.120
all the decisions based on the

00:19:01.121 --> 00:19:02.430
data that's in front of them,

00:19:02.431 --> 00:19:04.550
versus, but that's
not always the case.

