00:19:27.981 --> 00:19:31.285
In terms of the work that I lead,

00:19:31.286 --> 00:19:35.470
and I ask the academics

00:19:35.471 --> 00:19:37.980
or work with the academics
to undertake this,

00:19:37.981 --> 00:19:39.570
I've got three
multi-year research

00:19:39.571 --> 00:19:41.425
projects happening at present.

00:19:41.426 --> 00:19:45.860
And so GUARD, I think what
we did here was actually,

00:19:45.861 --> 00:19:48.130
get the acronym and then
figure out what the name of

00:19:48.131 --> 00:19:50.000
the project was retrospectively.

00:19:50.001 --> 00:19:52.595
Anyway, essentially GUARD is
about predicting conflicts.

00:19:52.596 --> 00:19:54.640
So, it's a combination of

00:19:54.641 --> 00:19:57.340
graph theory and
incorporating into

00:19:57.341 --> 00:19:59.710
those graphical based
representations

00:19:59.711 --> 00:20:02.090
some stochastic
differential equations,

00:20:02.091 --> 00:20:04.020
and model things as
a function of time.

00:20:04.021 --> 00:20:06.245
And integrating lots
of different datasets,

00:20:06.246 --> 00:20:07.825
to be able to predict

00:20:07.826 --> 00:20:10.125
areas that are
susceptible to conflict.

00:20:10.126 --> 00:20:12.250
And that could be, so we've been

00:20:12.251 --> 00:20:14.380
working with the Colombian
governments for instance,

00:20:14.381 --> 00:20:16.470
on looking at
different drugs cartels

00:20:16.471 --> 00:20:18.810
and how they interact,

00:20:18.811 --> 00:20:20.630
or how they shoot each other,

00:20:20.631 --> 00:20:23.010
and then suggest
intervention strategies based

00:20:23.011 --> 00:20:26.390
on the geographical topology,

00:20:26.391 --> 00:20:27.545
and some of the work
that we've been

00:20:27.546 --> 00:20:28.980
doing, and saying right okay,

00:20:28.981 --> 00:20:31.050
if you insert a roadblock
in this location and

00:20:31.051 --> 00:20:33.330
that's likely to
reduce the amount

00:20:33.331 --> 00:20:36.740
of shootings or interactions

00:20:36.741 --> 00:20:38.355
between these different groups,

00:20:38.356 --> 00:20:41.980
and all the way up to
the international levels in

00:20:41.981 --> 00:20:43.850
these types of
areas are likely to

00:20:43.851 --> 00:20:46.800
be susceptible to
conflicts in the future.

00:20:46.801 --> 00:20:48.780
Critically, because
I'm a Bayesian

00:20:48.781 --> 00:20:50.245
and obviously I applied

00:20:50.246 --> 00:20:51.570
prior distributions
over everything and we

00:20:51.571 --> 00:20:53.780
quantify the uncertainty
with everything that we do.

00:20:53.781 --> 00:20:55.820
Which is kind of cool.

00:20:55.821 --> 00:20:59.755
So, a project starts and
interaction between Warwick,

00:20:59.756 --> 00:21:02.470
so Turing Institute
Warwick University

00:21:02.471 --> 00:21:05.160
and University College London.

00:21:05.161 --> 00:21:09.425
AIDA is a bit like
DARPA's D3M project,

00:21:09.426 --> 00:21:15.375
but is on a completely
different financial scale,

00:21:15.376 --> 00:21:17.380
as you'd expect
because when DARPA

00:21:17.381 --> 00:21:19.500
invest they invest
big whereas when we

00:21:19.501 --> 00:21:21.685
invest we invest proportionately

00:21:21.686 --> 00:21:23.830
to how much money we
have got in the bank.

00:21:23.831 --> 00:21:25.180
So, in this project,

00:21:25.181 --> 00:21:26.845
there's an interaction
between Cambridge,

00:21:26.846 --> 00:21:29.110
Edinburgh and Oxford Universities

00:21:29.111 --> 00:21:30.695
and the Turing Institutes.

00:21:30.696 --> 00:21:32.550
And basically what we're
trying to do is to

00:21:32.551 --> 00:21:35.140
semi-automate parts of
the data wrangling process.

00:21:35.141 --> 00:21:38.990
So, we're looking at, is a lot
of Gaussian process stuff.

00:21:38.991 --> 00:21:40.890
If you've seen
Zoubin Ghahramani talk about

00:21:40.891 --> 00:21:43.535
the automated statistician and

00:21:43.536 --> 00:21:45.180
model selection in
Gaussian processes.

00:21:45.181 --> 00:21:46.290
Essentially that's kind of

00:21:46.291 --> 00:21:48.075
what we're doing in this space.

00:21:48.076 --> 00:21:51.880
And then finally, in
terms of, on this slide,

00:21:51.881 --> 00:21:53.480
computing in
untrusted environments,

00:21:53.481 --> 00:21:55.520
so what we did in
there is utilized in

00:21:55.521 --> 00:21:57.710
Intel's SGX, and technology.

00:21:57.711 --> 00:22:00.530
So we've we've just released

00:22:00.531 --> 00:22:05.830
a SGX compliant
Linux kind of library.

00:22:05.831 --> 00:22:07.075
So, SGX-LKL,

00:22:07.076 --> 00:22:10.280
or LKL-SGX, can't remember
which way around it is.

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:13.410
Sitting on top of that SGX

00:22:13.411 --> 00:22:15.010
compliant Linux kind of library

00:22:15.011 --> 00:22:17.155
we've actually placed Spark,

00:22:17.156 --> 00:22:19.660
and you may think that's
a trivial operation but

00:22:19.661 --> 00:22:22.530
the memory footprint
in SGX is quite small,

00:22:22.531 --> 00:22:24.700
and so the interactions
that one has to

00:22:24.701 --> 00:22:28.080
do to get Spark sit in,

00:22:28.081 --> 00:22:31.710
and even just the JVM
sitting inside of SGX,

00:22:31.711 --> 00:22:33.315
sitting in essentially
an encrypted memory,

00:22:33.316 --> 00:22:34.880
is quite significant and

00:22:34.881 --> 00:22:38.330
so what that has now given
us an ability to do,

00:22:38.331 --> 00:22:42.595
is to run secure
containers essentially,

00:22:42.596 --> 00:22:45.040
in cloud-based
infrastructure, and

00:22:45.041 --> 00:22:48.660
scale out using Spark's
scalability properties.

00:22:48.661 --> 00:22:51.125
Essentially we've got
full end-to-end encryption now,

00:22:51.126 --> 00:22:52.675
we've got encryption at rest,

00:22:52.676 --> 00:22:55.050
and we have encryption
using SGX when

00:22:55.051 --> 00:22:57.650
data and processing
capability is in memory.

00:22:57.651 --> 00:22:59.025
So, that's quite cool.

00:22:59.026 --> 00:23:00.865
So, we're partnered with them,

00:23:00.866 --> 00:23:03.280
that's Cambridge,
Imperial College,

00:23:03.281 --> 00:23:04.750
Turing Institutes and we have

00:23:04.751 --> 00:23:06.640
interactions with
