What we do in there is
based in deep learning,

00:23:36.821 --> 00:23:40.210
and not just because
I'm a Bayesian.

00:23:40.211 --> 00:23:41.360
Actually, the guy that run

00:23:41.361 --> 00:23:42.560
these projects is also Bayesian,

00:23:42.561 --> 00:23:46.040
so that's why I thought
that was a great idea.

00:23:46.041 --> 00:23:50.470
So, there's a natural
combination of things,

00:23:50.471 --> 00:23:52.360
but what we're doing there is

00:23:52.361 --> 00:23:54.620
essentially spotting
hours of distribution,

00:23:54.621 --> 00:23:59.270
data points, and
quantifying the probability

00:23:59.271 --> 00:24:01.350
that those kinds of data points

00:24:01.351 --> 00:24:04.930
are adversarial-related
or adversarily-generated.

00:24:04.931 --> 00:24:06.920
We've been doing some work

00:24:06.921 --> 00:24:08.600
with the National
Cybersecurity Center.

00:24:08.601 --> 00:24:13.750
So, GCHQ has an arm of GHCQ,

00:24:13.751 --> 00:24:15.400
which is called the National
Cybersecurity Center,

00:24:15.401 --> 00:24:17.350
and they're tasked with ensuring

00:24:17.351 --> 00:24:19.895
the UK is safe from
a cybersecurity perspective.

00:24:19.896 --> 00:24:22.500
So, we've been doing
some core work and run demark,

00:24:22.501 --> 00:24:24.734
analyzing demark data
and analyzing

00:24:24.735 --> 00:24:27.400
different interesting data sets

00:24:27.401 --> 00:24:32.720
to characterize the UK
governments web footprint.

00:24:32.721 --> 00:24:34.370
You may think that's
easy, but actually when

00:24:34.371 --> 00:24:37.830
a fire station in the middle
of the countryside,

00:24:37.831 --> 00:24:39.070
in the middle of
nowhere, in the UK,

00:24:39.071 --> 00:24:41.075
sets or pay websites on GoDaddy,

00:24:41.076 --> 00:24:43.000
or wherever, and pays them £20,

00:24:43.001 --> 00:24:44.910
you don't necessarily always know

00:24:44.911 --> 00:24:46.315
that that website's been created,

00:24:46.316 --> 00:24:47.910
and from a central
government perspective,

00:24:47.911 --> 00:24:49.360
yet you're responsible
for ensuring

00:24:49.361 --> 00:24:51.340
that the government
is responsible for

00:24:51.341 --> 00:24:55.205
ensuring that it's
protected to some extent.

00:24:55.206 --> 00:24:58.645
So, we've been trying to help
them to understand network.

00:24:58.646 --> 00:25:00.220
The final piece I'll touch upon

00:25:00.221 --> 00:25:03.750
is evaluating
homomorphic encryption.

00:25:03.751 --> 00:25:06.739
We've just open-sourced
a software platform,

00:25:06.740 --> 00:25:08.375
which we call SHEEP.

00:25:08.376 --> 00:25:11.280
I can't remember what
the acronym stands for,

00:25:11.281 --> 00:25:14.185
but it's one of
these most recursive acronyms.

00:25:14.186 --> 00:25:15.950
What that is, it's

00:25:15.951 --> 00:25:18.569
taken homomorphic
encryption primitives.

00:25:18.570 --> 00:25:21.060
So, the addition,
multiplication operations,

00:25:21.061 --> 00:25:22.620
and the different
implementations of those,

00:25:22.621 --> 00:25:25.170
and actually providing
a benchmarking platform,

00:25:25.171 --> 00:25:27.160
through which people when

00:25:27.161 --> 00:25:29.140
the clever
mathematicians generates

00:25:29.141 --> 00:25:33.050
a new FAG-related algorithm,

00:25:33.051 --> 00:25:34.960
they can push it on this
platform and they compare and

00:25:34.961 --> 00:25:37.490
contrast their
runtime performance,

00:25:37.491 --> 00:25:39.905
as well as some of the
mathematical assumptions that are

00:25:39.906 --> 00:25:43.330
baked into these
different algorithms.

00:25:43.960 --> 00:25:48.030
So, we noticed that in the
multiparty computation worlds,

00:25:48.031 --> 00:25:49.200
there's a benchmarking platform,

00:25:49.201 --> 00:25:52.280
but there wasn't one
in the FAG world,

00:25:52.281 --> 00:25:54.025
so we've now generated
this platform.

00:25:54.026 --> 00:25:56.930
There's a paper just ICML
on this platform.

00:25:56.931 --> 00:25:58.530
Hopefully, we'll see
some adoption of that.

00:25:58.531 --> 00:25:59.820
So, the reason I mentioned
that is, obviously,

00:25:59.821 --> 00:26:01.570
it's a bit of advertising
with thoughts of interest.

00:26:01.571 --> 00:26:03.075
If you're an FAG person,

00:26:03.076 --> 00:26:05.330
then please do have
a look at SHEEP

00:26:05.331 --> 00:26:09.075
and see whether it's
of interest to you.

00:26:09.076 --> 00:26:11.515
So, what I tried to do is

00:26:11.516 --> 00:26:15.490
cover not so quickly, so
I apologize for that.

00:26:15.491 --> 00:26:17.615
Quite a lot of the work that
we did at the Institute.

00:26:17.616 --> 00:26:19.010
I didn't really
touch upon some of

00:26:19.011 --> 00:26:20.260
the ethics side of things

00:26:20.261 --> 00:26:22.750
and some of the most social
sciences side of things,

00:26:22.751 --> 00:26:24.980
partly because that's not
my technical background.

00:26:24.981 --> 00:26:26.995
I would do them

00:26:26.996 --> 00:26:28.690
a disservice if I
tried to explain

00:26:28.691 --> 00:26:30.210
what we're doing in
the area of data ethics,

00:26:30.211 --> 00:26:32.695
but within the law on
that side of the spectrum.

00:26:32.696 --> 00:26:34.150
Working with lawyers, working

00:26:34.151 --> 00:26:37.760
with more of the kind
of philosophers,

00:26:37.761 --> 00:26:40.485
as it were trying to

00:26:40.486 --> 00:26:45.100
help the government and
organizations of different kinds,

00:26:45.101 --> 00:26:47.180
and show that they use data in

00:26:47.181 --> 00:26:49.505
an ethical and legally
compliant manner,

00:26:49.506 --> 00:26:51.890
and changing the UK law such

00:26:51.891 --> 00:26:55.250
that we are ethically
responsible in the use of data.

00:26:55.251 --> 00:26:56.920
That's quite an interesting area

00:26:56.921 --> 00:26:58.210
from a cybersecurity perspective.

00:26:58.211 --> 00:26:59.220
There is something that I've

00:26:59.221 --> 00:27:00.940
recently been thinking about.

00:27:00.941 --> 00:27:03.535
So, I wouldn't say anything
profound at the moment,

00:27:03.536 --> 00:27:05.855
but I think it's
an area that we should,

00:27:05.856 --> 00:27:08.400
for those of you that are
cybersecurity related research,

00:27:08.401 --> 00:27:09.960
you should start to think a

00:27:09.961 --> 00:27:12.255
little bit more than we
currently perhaps do.

00:27:12.256 --> 00:27:14.440
So, I'll start the segue
now into a bit more

00:27:14.441 --> 00:27:17.770
of a technical content.

00:27:17.810 --> 00:27:21.290
So, I was interested,

00:27:21.291 --> 00:27:23.460
so I joined the Alan Turing
Institute for many reasons,

00:27:23.461 --> 00:27:26.760
partly because it's
a national institute in the UK,

00:27:26.761 --> 00:27:29.315
and partly because of the brand,

00:27:29.316 --> 00:27:31.225
the brand of the man himself,

00:27:31.226 --> 00:27:33.845
and the brand of
the university partners

00:27:33.846 --> 00:27:35.160
and the institute essentially

00:27:35.161 --> 00:27:37.005
trades off all of those brands.

00:27:37.006 --> 00:27:38.510
That's what gives us

00:27:38.511 --> 00:27:40.220
great convening power and

00:27:40.221 --> 00:27:42.665
also allows us to create
national level impacts.

00:27:42.666 --> 00:27:46.640
I was interested in
Alan Turing and what he did,

00:27:46.641 --> 00:27:48.610
so I started to
read some papers of

00:27:48.611 --> 00:27:51.635
his and papers that
were written about him.

00:27:51.636 --> 00:27:55.430
It turns out, you may
suspect I would say this,

00:27:55.431 --> 00:27:59.390
that Alan Turing himself
and I'll cast him,

00:27:59.391 --> 00:28:01.020
perhaps maybe not one
of the first British,

00:28:01.021 --> 00:28:04.800
certainly one of
the first data scientists

00:28:04.801 --> 00:28:06.055
that I've come across.

00:28:06.056 --> 00:28:10.425
That's through my limited
reading in history.

00:28:10.426 --> 00:28:13.290
He was a statistical
data scientist,

00:28:13.291 --> 00:28:16.190
specifically at Bletchley Park.
Why do I say that?

00:28:16.191 --> 00:28:17.760
Well, I'll give
you some citations

00:28:17.761 --> 00:28:18.940
and some quotes from him

00:28:18.941 --> 00:28:22.110
to demonstrate that
that's partly true.

00:28:22.111 --> 00:28:24.950
But if data science
is the combination

00:28:24.951 --> 00:28:26.220
of different
scientific disciplines

00:28:26.221 --> 00:28:28.155
to derive value from data,

00:28:28.156 --> 00:28:30.330
then Turing and his work

00:28:30.331 --> 00:28:31.970
at Bletchley Park, essentially
that's what he did,

00:28:31.971 --> 00:28:35.520
so in Hut eight at Bletchley
Park, he had linguists.

00:28:35.521 --> 00:28:38.230
He had, I guess
the first computer scientists.

00:28:38.231 --> 00:28:39.770
He had hardware engineers.

00:28:39.771 --> 00:28:43.169
Quite crucially, he had
Jack Good as a statistician,

00:28:43.170 --> 00:28:46.450
which was helping to guide
him towards the boundaries,

00:28:46.451 --> 00:28:47.700
MAS algorithm, all the work they

00:28:47.701 --> 00:28:50.270
did in deciphering Enigma.

00:28:50.271 --> 00:28:54.895
So, I propose that, or rather,

00:28:54.896 --> 00:28:57.190
the literature and I proposed

00:28:57.191 --> 00:28:59.240
that Turing was
a statistical data scientist,

00:28:59.241 --> 00:29:00.730
so the institute has

00:29:00.731 --> 00:29:04.780
quite a natural link back
to Turing and his work.

00:29:04.781 --> 00:29:06.160
Then, if we focus in

00:29:06.161 --> 00:29:09.025
on Alan Turing's view
of probability.

00:29:09.026 --> 00:29:13.445
So, there is a really cool paper.

00:29:13.446 --> 00:29:17.330
Back in 2012, Alan Turing
appeared on archive,

00:29:17.331 --> 00:29:20.310
which is not something
that happens every day.

00:29:20.610 --> 00:29:23.600
When you get notifications
on you that Turing's just

00:29:23.601 --> 00:29:26.905
appeared on archive,
but GCHQ kindly,

00:29:26.906 --> 00:29:29.050
openly published, declassified,
and openly published

00:29:29.051 --> 00:29:32.550
a paper of Turing's in 2012.

00:29:32.551 --> 00:29:33.730
You can go on
the Internet and download

00:29:33.731 --> 00:29:35.230
this either from archive because

00:29:35.231 --> 00:29:36.550
somebody's typed it
up where you can see

00:29:36.551 --> 00:29:39.340
the original manuscripts,
which was handwritten.

00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:42.710
The paper by Turing,

00:29:42.711 --> 00:29:44.430
which was written in 1941,

00:29:44.431 --> 00:29:46.161
but suddenly published in 2012,

00:29:46.162 --> 00:29:47.110
it's titled

00:29:47.111 --> 00:29:49.700
The Application of
Probability to Cryptography.

00:29:49.701 --> 00:29:52.450
My mathematical knowledge,
being a statistician,

00:29:52.451 --> 00:29:54.950
isn't as great as
I'd like it to be.

00:29:55.420 --> 00:29:57.600
Well, I actually do

00:29:57.601 --> 00:29:59.560
understand the cryptography
that goes on in this paper,

00:29:59.561 --> 00:30:01.205
but I don't fully
understand cryptography

00:30:01.206 --> 00:30:03.260
in any meaningful sense,

00:30:03.261 --> 00:30:05.240
but I do understand
the probability side

00:30:05.241 --> 00:30:07.675
of things that he
presented, thankfully.

00:30:07.676 --> 00:30:10.925
Some of the key quotes
that come from this paper.

00:30:10.926 --> 00:30:14.290
Actually, there's a great guy,

00:30:14.291 --> 00:30:18.110
a great US-based academic
head called Sandy Zabell,

00:30:18.111 --> 00:30:20.440
who has done quite a lot
of work on the history of

00:30:20.441 --> 00:30:24.065
Turing and provided
commentary on this paper,

00:30:24.066 --> 00:30:25.560
which so if you're
interested in this paper,

00:30:25.561 --> 00:30:27.740
I suggest you also read
this accompanying paper,

00:30:27.741 --> 00:30:29.545
which provides a commentary.

00:30:29.546 --> 00:30:31.020
It's actually Sandy Zabell

00:30:31.021 --> 00:30:32.835
that pulled out some
of these quotes.

00:30:32.836 --> 00:30:35.430
So, this, to me,

00:30:35.431 --> 00:30:40.240
is evidence that
Turing was a Bayesian,

00:30:40.241 --> 00:30:43.590
is a Bayesian, was
a Bayesian, I guess.

00:30:43.610 --> 00:30:46.500
The use of Bayesian statistics

00:30:46.501 --> 00:30:49.505
through World War II was actually

00:30:49.506 --> 00:30:51.490
the key thing that
helps us to decipher

00:30:51.491 --> 00:30:55.070
Nick Moon allegedly win the war.

00:30:55.071 --> 00:30:57.375
So, on Bayesian statistics,

00:30:57.376 --> 00:30:59.880
if I extrapolate
Bayesian statistics

00:30:59.881 --> 00:31:01.620
and Bayesian methodology
helped to win the war.

00:31:01.621 --> 00:31:03.360
That's quite
extraordinary, given that

00:31:03.361 --> 00:31:04.730
Bayesian statistics in the '40s

00:31:04.731 --> 00:31:06.955
was seen as something
that one should never do.

00:31:06.956 --> 00:31:10.015
So from my heart,

00:31:10.016 --> 00:31:11.150
it's further off to

00:31:11.151 --> 00:31:13.240
Alan Turing for actually
persevering with

00:31:13.241 --> 00:31:16.010
the Bayesian methodology
in utilizing this

00:31:16.011 --> 00:31:17.080
in the way that I'll

00:31:17.081 --> 00:31:19.075
describe in a moment
released at high level.

00:31:19.076 --> 00:31:21.380
Turing said in his manuscript,

00:31:21.381 --> 00:31:22.825
the probability of an event

00:31:22.826 --> 00:31:24.420
on certain evidence
is the proportion of

00:31:24.421 --> 00:31:26.175
cases in that which an event

00:31:26.176 --> 00:31:28.090
may be expected to happen
given that evidence.

00:31:28.091 --> 00:31:30.060
So, that loosely suggests
that he's a Bayesian.

00:31:30.061 --> 00:31:31.500
He's thinking about
conditional probabilities.

00:31:31.501 --> 00:31:33.325
That's my interpretation
of that statement.

00:31:33.326 --> 00:31:34.680
But then more specifically,

00:31:34.681 --> 00:31:35.970
he talks about the evidence

00:31:35.971 --> 00:31:37.460
concerning the
possibility of an event

00:31:37.461 --> 00:31:38.960
occurring usually
divides into a part

00:31:38.961 --> 00:31:41.080
about which statistics
are available,

00:31:41.081 --> 00:31:43.245
i.e., a likelihood function,

00:31:43.246 --> 00:31:46.260
under less definite parts
about which one

00:31:46.261 --> 00:31:49.260
can only use one's judgments,
i.e prior knowledge.

00:31:49.261 --> 00:31:51.310
We combine those in
a mathematically rigorous way,

00:31:51.311 --> 00:31:52.450
as you all know, because I'm sure

00:31:52.451 --> 00:31:53.820
some of you are Bayesians
in this audience.

00:31:53.821 --> 00:31:58.400
Well, I hope you are. We
get posterior distribution.

00:31:58.401 --> 00:32:02.135
So, that suggests
Turing was Bayesian.

00:32:02.136 --> 00:32:04.200
Actually, what really
suggest Turing was

00:32:04.201 --> 00:32:08.210
a Bayesian was the quotes which

00:32:08.211 --> 00:32:09.720
directly states

00:32:09.721 --> 00:32:11.430
that nearly all applications
of probability to

00:32:11.431 --> 00:32:13.090
cryptography depend

00:32:13.091 --> 00:32:15.140
on the factor principle
or Bayes theorem.

00:32:15.141 --> 00:32:17.510
So, back in 1940's,

00:32:17.511 --> 00:32:19.400
Bayesian statistics
have been used.

00:32:19.401 --> 00:32:22.930
Essentially, what we're computing

00:32:22.931 --> 00:32:25.380
was Bayes factors and

00:32:25.381 --> 00:32:29.025
we introduce some cool
computational tools, the deciban.

00:32:29.026 --> 00:32:31.650
Deciban was introduced
basically in the same way that,

00:32:31.651 --> 00:32:33.260
the computer scientists
in the audience

00:32:33.261 --> 00:32:34.580
was used computational tricks

00:32:34.581 --> 00:32:38.675
to improve computational
tractability.

00:32:38.676 --> 00:32:40.130
The deciban was introduced

00:32:40.131 --> 00:32:42.010
back in the 40's to
do exactly that.

00:32:42.011 --> 00:32:46.460
So, some cool things that we
do now almost naturally as

00:32:46.461 --> 00:32:48.350
a data science community
that they would do in

00:32:48.351 --> 00:32:49.820
Hut eight back in the

00:32:49.821 --> 00:32:53.320
1940's which I find
quite inspirational.

00:32:53.321 --> 00:32:57.650
Again, there's another
great paper by Jack Good.

00:32:57.651 --> 00:32:59.730
This stuff, the
work that they were

00:32:59.731 --> 00:33:01.630
doing in Hut eight
really started to not

00:33:01.631 --> 00:33:03.290
leak but started to appear in

00:33:03.291 --> 00:33:06.915
the open literature
around 1980's.

00:33:06.916 --> 00:33:10.140
Again, Jack Good, I have

00:33:10.141 --> 00:33:12.480
infinite respect for these
kinds of academics who

00:33:12.481 --> 00:33:14.770
were utilizing big proponents of

00:33:14.771 --> 00:33:16.000
these kinds of methodologies that

00:33:16.001 --> 00:33:18.225
were completely out of favor.

00:33:18.226 --> 00:33:20.490
During the period,

00:33:20.491 --> 00:33:21.550
and during the 40s,

00:33:21.551 --> 00:33:22.715
and yet they persevered.

00:33:22.716 --> 00:33:25.460
They knew that this
changed the course of

00:33:25.461 --> 00:33:27.780
the war in specific ways

00:33:27.781 --> 00:33:29.770
in which they utilized
relative simple ways,

00:33:29.771 --> 00:33:31.464
in which they utilized
these methodologies,

00:33:31.465 --> 00:33:32.780
and they were constantly told by

00:33:32.781 --> 00:33:34.050
the rest of the academic
community that

00:33:34.051 --> 00:33:36.750
Bayes in statistics was
just not useful in any way,

00:33:36.751 --> 00:33:38.770
shape or form, and
yet they had to keep,

00:33:38.771 --> 00:33:40.350
that's bite the lip and not

00:33:40.351 --> 00:33:42.120
actually release
any of this stuff.

00:33:42.121 --> 00:33:43.890
So, it's remarkable.

00:33:43.891 --> 00:33:46.320
I'm not sure I would have
the self-control to be able

00:33:46.321 --> 00:33:48.700
to not leak all

00:33:48.701 --> 00:33:50.670
secret. Maybe, I shouldn't
say that actually.

00:33:50.671 --> 00:33:54.155
I have the self-control, just
in case anybody's watching.

00:33:54.156 --> 00:34:02.395
So, that leads me to take
on my interest which is,

00:34:02.396 --> 00:34:05.210
I want to be able to build

00:34:05.211 --> 00:34:08.530
a Bayesian model for
cyber-security or,

00:34:08.531 --> 00:34:09.830
specifically in

00:34:09.831 --> 00:34:12.430
this context network-based
cyber-security.

00:34:12.431 --> 00:34:15.730
I think there are four key things

00:34:15.731 --> 00:34:16.920
that we need to learn about,

00:34:16.921 --> 00:34:17.695
and I have not done
any of these yet.

00:34:17.696 --> 00:34:20.070
I should say that in writing,
beginning this journey.

00:34:20.071 --> 00:34:21.660
I might be interested
in anybody's

00:34:21.661 --> 00:34:22.680
thoughts in any of these things,

00:34:22.681 --> 00:34:24.205
and I'll allude to

00:34:24.206 --> 00:34:25.840
the problems that sits
within these things.

00:34:25.841 --> 00:34:29.490
So, I think the key
ingredients are as follows;

00:34:29.491 --> 00:34:31.775
so Chain Event Graph essentially,

00:34:31.776 --> 00:34:35.505
a way in which one
can undertake causal,

00:34:35.506 --> 00:34:37.050
specifically Bayesian
analysis but

00:34:37.051 --> 00:34:38.920
causal statistical analysis,

00:34:38.921 --> 00:34:42.935
and represents the causality
between events using game.

00:34:42.936 --> 00:34:45.650
Event trees, and this is a
general Chain Event Graphs.

00:34:45.651 --> 00:34:47.240
So, a generalization
of event trees,

00:34:47.241 --> 00:34:49.150
for those of you that
don't know them,

00:34:49.151 --> 00:34:53.810
and eliciting great
expertise from people like

00:34:53.811 --> 00:34:56.610
Johnson and others about

00:34:56.611 --> 00:34:57.960
cyber-security systems

00:34:57.961 --> 00:34:59.380
by adversaries about
how they work,

00:34:59.381 --> 00:35:00.460
about systems of systems,

00:35:00.461 --> 00:35:02.210
about supply chains et cetera,

00:35:02.211 --> 00:35:03.785
building all of that into

00:35:03.786 --> 00:35:05.490
quite a complicated statistical

00:35:05.491 --> 00:35:07.810
causal Bayesian model using

00:35:07.811 --> 00:35:10.695
this Chain Event Graph
methodological system

00:35:10.696 --> 00:35:13.145
to represent what's going
on in the real world.

00:35:13.146 --> 00:35:14.329
So, that's the incorporation

00:35:14.330 --> 00:35:16.795
of prior knowledge as it were.

00:35:16.796 --> 00:35:19.780
So, I'm particularly interested
in Chain Event Graphs,

00:35:19.781 --> 00:35:22.780
so I just started a project
which is actually

00:35:22.781 --> 00:35:26.480
looking not
cyber-security at all,

00:35:26.481 --> 00:35:27.800
but I'm hoping it will
translate into that.

00:35:27.801 --> 00:35:30.300
So, I've started a project
using Chain Event Graphs,

00:35:30.301 --> 00:35:34.070
which is looking at how
an individual will go

00:35:34.071 --> 00:35:38.830
from minor radicalization
to full radicalization,

00:35:38.831 --> 00:35:43.325
to taking part in
some terrorist related attack in

00:35:43.326 --> 00:35:45.660
Western country and looking at

00:35:45.661 --> 00:35:48.345
the sociological
evolution as it were,

00:35:48.346 --> 00:35:50.675
an individual's
psychological and the

00:35:50.676 --> 00:35:54.210
sociological surroundings
evolution through

00:35:54.211 --> 00:35:57.100
that path from being somebody
like myself all the way

00:35:57.101 --> 00:35:58.110
through to somebody being very

00:35:58.111 --> 00:36:00.465
radicalized and now I'm
performing in stack.

00:36:00.466 --> 00:36:02.830
That has analogies in

00:36:02.831 --> 00:36:06.355
some respects to
the cyber-security kill chain.

00:36:06.356 --> 00:36:08.020
So, I'm hoping that

00:36:08.021 --> 00:36:09.670
the learnings from
that work will translate

00:36:09.671 --> 00:36:12.970
across into the cyber world.

00:36:12.971 --> 00:36:16.490
So, we've got the ability

00:36:16.491 --> 00:36:20.160
to do causal inference assuming
that this stuff works.

00:36:20.161 --> 00:36:21.710
The next thing I'm interested in

00:36:21.711 --> 00:36:25.680
is horizontally
scalable inference.

00:36:25.681 --> 00:36:27.630
So, I am particularly

00:36:27.631 --> 00:36:30.080
interested in scalable
statistical methods.

00:36:30.081 --> 00:36:33.220
So, things that sit on top
of platforms such as Spark.

00:36:33.221 --> 00:36:36.920
What I see, a lot of the time in

00:36:36.921 --> 00:36:38.870
Spark-related applications
is that people

00:36:38.871 --> 00:36:41.010
like to count, and that's great.

00:36:41.011 --> 00:36:42.975
We do a lot of summarization

00:36:42.976 --> 00:36:44.975
through counting a lot of things.

00:36:44.976 --> 00:36:48.000
But that's not
the most sophisticated one

00:36:48.001 --> 00:36:50.520
can do with respect
to statistics.

00:36:50.521 --> 00:36:53.970
So, what I want to do is to get

00:36:53.971 --> 00:36:56.610
most statistical sophistication

00:36:56.611 --> 00:36:59.650
to Spark-like environments.

00:36:59.651 --> 00:37:02.400
So, we're currently
starting a project,

00:37:02.401 --> 00:37:05.045
an open-source projects
that complements Spark ML,

00:37:05.046 --> 00:37:09.440
MLlib, sorry, which is
looking at place in MCMC,

00:37:09.441 --> 00:37:10.850
Markov Chain Monte Carlo related

00:37:10.851 --> 00:37:13.100
algorithms on top of Spark,

00:37:13.101 --> 00:37:15.900
and so there's some
interesting challenges that

00:37:15.901 --> 00:37:19.330
exist when you're
trying to perform MCMC.

00:37:19.331 --> 00:37:21.420
You've got distributed datasets
across multiple machines,

00:37:21.421 --> 00:37:23.120
you're running
local MCMC algorithms

00:37:23.121 --> 00:37:25.130
computing local
posterior distributions,

00:37:25.131 --> 00:37:26.940
or sample-based representations

00:37:26.941 --> 00:37:28.110
of those posterior distributions.

00:37:28.111 --> 00:37:30.470
How do you combine those
samples to produce

00:37:30.471 --> 00:37:32.990
a globally consistent and

00:37:32.991 --> 00:37:36.730
statistically correct
MCMC algorithm

00:37:36.731 --> 00:37:38.655
or rather representation up

00:37:38.656 --> 00:37:40.500
asymptotically correct
representation

00:37:40.501 --> 00:37:42.090
of the posterior distribution,

00:37:42.091 --> 00:37:44.135
and that's what this
project's all about.

00:37:44.136 --> 00:37:46.230
So, we just started
quite a big project as

00:37:46.231 --> 00:37:49.440
a collaboration
between Cambridge,

00:37:49.570 --> 00:37:52.015
Warwick, Oxford and Bristol,

00:37:52.016 --> 00:37:55.260
and the Turing Institute
on just that topic.

00:37:55.261 --> 00:37:57.600
The best thing we've come up
with so far is quite a kill,

00:37:57.601 --> 00:37:59.590
rejection sampling algorithm,

00:37:59.591 --> 00:38:01.760
which is this perfect
sampling all kinds of things.

00:38:01.761 --> 00:38:03.920
We'll be publishing
that very shortly.

00:38:03.921 --> 00:38:10.000
Computationally, it's horrendous.
It's just some Spark.

00:38:10.001 --> 00:38:12.500
So, we have the
horizontal scalability.

00:38:12.501 --> 00:38:15.190
It takes quite a while just
to do one MCMC iterations,

00:38:15.191 --> 00:38:16.040
so there's still quite a lot of

00:38:16.041 --> 00:38:17.990
research to do in this space

00:38:17.991 --> 00:38:19.510
but we do have ideas

00:38:19.511 --> 00:38:22.300
