00:44:15.771 --> 00:44:19.315
So, given NetFlow data,

00:44:19.316 --> 00:44:20.750
I have given, metadatas are

00:44:20.751 --> 00:44:22.790
about the packets flying
across the network.

00:44:22.791 --> 00:44:26.620
What I want to do is just
analyze an individual device,

00:44:26.621 --> 00:44:29.720
and I want to [inaudible]
use of behavior.

00:44:29.721 --> 00:44:30.960
So, I want to know, just from

00:44:30.961 --> 00:44:33.860
the timing information of
the events on the computer,

00:44:33.861 --> 00:44:36.635
can I figure out what
the user was doing,

00:44:36.636 --> 00:44:38.075
were they streaming,
were they are on,

00:44:38.076 --> 00:44:39.150
not necessarily were
they on YouTube,

00:44:39.151 --> 00:44:40.690
but were they streaming video,

00:44:40.691 --> 00:44:43.080
were they writing emails,

00:44:43.081 --> 00:44:45.095
were they doing nothing at all.

00:44:45.096 --> 00:44:47.405
What kinds of activity
were they doing?

00:44:47.406 --> 00:44:51.410
So, I want to come in further
using a Bayesian process.

00:44:51.411 --> 00:44:53.365
The Bayesian process
I've chose to use

00:44:53.366 --> 00:44:56.550
is a Markov Modulated
Poisson Process.

00:44:57.090 --> 00:44:59.695
What is an MMPP?

00:44:59.696 --> 00:45:04.120
Well, really simply,
what we've got is a,

00:45:04.121 --> 00:45:06.900
this is some other kind
of more formal detail,

00:45:06.901 --> 00:45:11.670
but I won't go through this
in the interest of time.

00:45:12.460 --> 00:45:15.235
What we have is a hidden,

00:45:15.236 --> 00:45:17.905
a latent continuous
time Markov chain,

00:45:17.906 --> 00:45:20.080
which is denoting or

00:45:20.081 --> 00:45:25.430
rather representing
the user behavior.

00:45:25.431 --> 00:45:27.130
So, we've got a finite state,

00:45:27.131 --> 00:45:29.465
continuous-time Markov chain.

00:45:29.466 --> 00:45:31.620
So, if the chain
is in state zero,

00:45:31.621 --> 00:45:35.130
the user's inactive and if
the state is in chain one.

00:45:35.131 --> 00:45:36.615
If the chain is in state one,

00:45:36.616 --> 00:45:39.540
then perhaps the wrong
video streaming websites,

00:45:39.541 --> 00:45:40.560
and so on and so forth.

00:45:40.561 --> 00:45:42.410
So, we have some
interesting challenges

00:45:42.411 --> 00:45:43.260
that are both in terms of

00:45:43.261 --> 00:45:44.340
specifying the number of states.

00:45:44.341 --> 00:45:45.885
We've got a model
selection problem,

00:45:45.886 --> 00:45:48.150
linking it back to
Alan Turing's work in

00:45:48.151 --> 00:45:50.600
model selection and base factors
and all that good stuff.

00:45:50.601 --> 00:45:52.185
So once we solve that problem,

00:45:52.186 --> 00:45:53.350
then we have an
inference problem.

00:45:53.351 --> 00:45:56.075
We want to infer the state,

00:45:56.076 --> 00:45:58.060
what are they doing
on the computer.

00:45:58.061 --> 00:45:59.590
I know all our
likelihood function

00:45:59.591 --> 00:46:01.075
is essentially
a point process model,

00:46:01.076 --> 00:46:03.655
a plus one point process model,

00:46:03.656 --> 00:46:07.140
where we get the
event timing data.

00:46:07.141 --> 00:46:08.710
From event timing data,
we want to infer

00:46:08.711 --> 00:46:11.845
this state of this
continuous-time Markov chain,

00:46:11.846 --> 00:46:17.020
and some stuff on
continuous time Markov chains,

00:46:17.021 --> 00:46:18.670
which I will skip over

00:46:18.671 --> 00:46:21.140
because you're either interested
in it, and you know it.

00:46:21.141 --> 00:46:22.415
Or, you're not interested in it,

00:46:22.416 --> 00:46:24.275
and it's doormats that
nobody really cares

00:46:24.276 --> 00:46:27.620
about, interesting math,
nevertheless.

00:46:31.110 --> 00:46:33.845
So, the kind of
key contribution that we made.

00:46:33.846 --> 00:46:36.815
The first one was from
an application perspective.

00:46:36.816 --> 00:46:38.675
We were able to kind of,

00:46:38.676 --> 00:46:39.960
as you'll see on the next slide,

00:46:39.961 --> 00:46:42.870
we were able to
demonstrate that one can

00:46:42.871 --> 00:46:46.200
infer some stuff from using
these types of methodologies.

00:46:46.201 --> 00:46:47.660
The second contribution that we

00:46:47.661 --> 00:46:50.965
made was in terms of
parameter estimation,

00:46:50.966 --> 00:46:53.620
and so from a
methodological perspective,

00:46:53.621 --> 00:46:55.710
and not continuous-time
Markov chain.

00:46:55.711 --> 00:46:57.115
What you want to do is to

00:46:57.116 --> 00:47:00.500
estimate a bunch of
parameters or two parameters,

00:47:00.501 --> 00:47:02.580
which are reasonable
dimension depending on

00:47:02.581 --> 00:47:04.330
the number of hidden states

00:47:04.331 --> 00:47:06.580
that you have in this
continuous-time Markov chain.

00:47:06.581 --> 00:47:09.010
You can do that using
EM or Gibbs sampling,

00:47:09.011 --> 00:47:10.855
so it's all relatively
straightforward.

00:47:10.856 --> 00:47:13.830
But, from an estimation
perspective,

00:47:13.831 --> 00:47:15.420
what you need to be able to do,

00:47:15.421 --> 00:47:19.875
is to construct
the smooth distribution.

00:47:19.876 --> 00:47:22.520
So, if your state
space modelling,

00:47:22.521 --> 00:47:24.895
if you are trying to compute

00:47:24.896 --> 00:47:26.180
the posterior distribution as

00:47:26.181 --> 00:47:29.015
a function of time as you
get more and more data.

00:47:29.016 --> 00:47:31.445
What you need to be able
to do is to compute

00:47:31.446 --> 00:47:35.480
this distribution
conditional on all the data.

00:47:35.481 --> 00:47:38.140
I've omitted lots
and lots of details,

00:47:38.141 --> 00:47:39.580
puts in the accompanying paper.

00:47:39.581 --> 00:47:44.690
What we realized is that,

00:47:44.691 --> 00:47:46.930
when you're computing
the smoothing distribution,

00:47:46.931 --> 00:47:49.900
you run a filter forward
across the data in time.

00:47:49.901 --> 00:47:51.320
You want to filter
backwards in time,

00:47:51.321 --> 00:47:52.510
and you combine the outputs

00:47:52.511 --> 00:47:53.775
of the different points in time.

00:47:53.776 --> 00:47:55.130
This backwards filter is actually

00:47:55.131 --> 00:47:56.515
not a probability measure.

00:47:56.516 --> 00:47:59.365
It needn't be necessarily
a finite measure.

00:47:59.366 --> 00:48:00.814
So, when you use

00:48:00.815 --> 00:48:02.730
sequential Monte-Carlo
related techniques,

00:48:02.731 --> 00:48:04.590
which you have to use
in this particular case

00:48:04.591 --> 00:48:06.140
and to estimate
these distributions,

00:48:06.141 --> 00:48:08.920
because the solution [inaudible]

00:48:08.921 --> 00:48:11.595
admit none of the analytically
tractable solution.

00:48:11.596 --> 00:48:14.320
This estimation of this measure,

00:48:14.321 --> 00:48:15.920
which is not necessarily
a finite measure,

00:48:15.921 --> 00:48:17.170
means that these techniques,

00:48:17.171 --> 00:48:19.630
all the convergence theory
goes out to the window.

00:48:19.631 --> 00:48:22.050
So the methodological
contribution that we

00:48:22.051 --> 00:48:24.760
made in this particular paper was

00:48:24.761 --> 00:48:28.615
to guarantee that
the backward filter

00:48:28.616 --> 00:48:29.805
is a finite measure.

00:48:29.806 --> 00:48:32.400
The very least by introducing
an artificial distribution,

00:48:32.401 --> 00:48:35.135
we did some probabilistic
manipulations

00:48:35.136 --> 00:48:36.940
to kind of remove

00:48:36.941 --> 00:48:38.070
the effects of
some artificial distribution

00:48:38.071 --> 00:48:40.140
that we introduced and make

00:48:40.141 --> 00:48:42.530
this whole system computable

00:48:42.531 --> 00:48:44.920
using the types of methodologies,

00:48:44.921 --> 00:48:46.625
sequential Monte-Carlo
we have to use,

00:48:46.626 --> 00:48:48.270
which is then embedded
in a parameter

00:48:48.271 --> 00:48:50.770
estimation algorithm to
estimate the parameter.

00:48:50.771 --> 00:48:54.115
Parameter estimation is very

00:48:54.116 --> 00:48:55.410
computationally expensive

00:48:55.411 --> 00:48:56.680
once you estimate the parameters,

00:48:56.681 --> 00:48:58.370
the actual algorithm
to infer this stuff,

00:48:58.371 --> 00:48:59.995
to infer the states of the user,

00:48:59.996 --> 00:49:01.735
is actually relatively
straightforward.

00:49:01.736 --> 00:49:03.370
So, just to kind of reiterate,

00:49:03.371 --> 00:49:04.540
we got point process data.

00:49:04.541 --> 00:49:08.800
We want to estimate the states
of this hidden state,

00:49:08.801 --> 00:49:11.670
how you actually use
the [inaudible].

00:49:11.671 --> 00:49:12.720
We use a relatively simple

00:49:12.721 --> 00:49:14.435
algorithm to be able to do that.

00:49:14.436 --> 00:49:16.380
That's Markov modulated
Poisson process algorithm to

00:49:16.381 --> 00:49:17.600
estimate the probability
of the states

00:49:17.601 --> 00:49:18.920
in any one particular time.

00:49:18.921 --> 00:49:20.200
Prior to that, we
have to estimate

00:49:20.201 --> 00:49:22.710
the parameters of
the system we use,

00:49:22.711 --> 00:49:24.420
in our case, we used
Gibbs sampling.

00:49:24.421 --> 00:49:25.880
But within that Gibbs
sampling procedure,

00:49:25.881 --> 00:49:28.085
we needed a sequential
Monte-Carlo algorithm,

00:49:28.086 --> 00:49:29.380
and we realized that there was

00:49:29.381 --> 00:49:32.450
this technical problem about
this backwards filter,

00:49:32.451 --> 00:49:35.350
not necessarily approximating
a finite measure,

00:49:35.351 --> 00:49:38.750
and so we solve that
particular problem as well.

00:49:38.751 --> 00:49:40.490
So, that's the
methodological contribution,

00:49:40.491 --> 00:49:43.580
then gets applied in
cyber security context.

00:49:43.581 --> 00:49:45.640
In this particular case,

00:49:45.641 --> 00:49:46.890
I think we went for

00:49:46.891 --> 00:49:50.615
