on what's on the network.

00:57:41.331 --> 00:57:42.430
They may obviously, not

00:57:42.431 --> 00:57:43.865
always know what's
on the network.

00:57:43.866 --> 00:57:47.720
But I think, having
those interactions you

00:57:47.721 --> 00:57:49.745
can then better model the world

00:57:49.746 --> 00:57:50.980
and incorporate the uncertainty

00:57:50.981 --> 00:57:52.420
associated with those models.

00:57:52.421 --> 00:57:55.995
So again, how I can replicate
the Bayesian methodology.

00:57:55.996 --> 00:57:57.330
I feel like I've hammered

00:57:57.331 --> 00:57:59.095
that point a little bit
too much today.

00:57:59.096 --> 00:58:00.750
I am pragmatic most of the time,

00:58:00.751 --> 00:58:03.340
but as long as
the Bayesian solution.

00:58:05.570 --> 00:58:10.425
&gt;&gt; So, you talked about using

00:58:10.426 --> 00:58:12.670
Polymorphic Encryption
and actually

00:58:12.671 --> 00:58:13.990
I'm impressed that someone

00:58:13.991 --> 00:58:15.320
got logistic progression
to work there.

00:58:15.321 --> 00:58:16.520
It seems like should
be really hard

00:58:16.521 --> 00:58:21.560
because exponentiation
is not a ring operation.

00:58:22.880 --> 00:58:26.640
But you can say you
can evaluate using

00:58:26.641 --> 00:58:28.235
polymorphic encryption
if you want to

00:58:28.236 --> 00:58:30.380
do this in some privacy
preserving way.

00:58:30.381 --> 00:58:32.450
Of course, then you
would have the answer to

00:58:32.451 --> 00:58:35.180
your thing you
evaluated for someone,

00:58:35.181 --> 00:58:36.740
but you can only do that if

00:58:36.741 --> 00:58:38.210
you get the model from somewhere

00:58:38.211 --> 00:58:40.850
and I was wondering if you had

00:58:40.851 --> 00:58:42.020
any thoughts about
how that might be

00:58:42.021 --> 00:58:43.915
done in a privacy preserving way.

00:58:43.916 --> 00:58:46.930
There is hope that one
might be able to build

00:58:46.931 --> 00:58:49.705
a model using encrypted data,

00:58:49.706 --> 00:58:51.880
but it's always off
and the data has all

00:58:51.881 --> 00:58:53.200
been encrypted with the same key

00:58:53.201 --> 00:58:54.830
in all the schemes I've heard.

00:58:54.831 --> 00:58:57.540
&gt;&gt; So, no is the short answer.

00:58:57.541 --> 00:59:00.060
Because, I rely on

00:59:00.061 --> 00:59:03.900
my colleagues to do
that work for me or with me.

00:59:03.901 --> 00:59:06.080
So, no I don't have a sensible
answer to that question.

00:59:06.081 --> 00:59:08.470
So, I'll avoid
the question completely.

00:59:08.471 --> 00:59:10.110
Hopefully, if any of
my colleagues are

00:59:10.111 --> 00:59:11.630
watching then they
can email me and say,

00:59:11.631 --> 00:59:12.400
"This is what the answer to

00:59:12.401 --> 00:59:13.420
that question should have been."

00:59:13.421 --> 00:59:13.780
&gt;&gt; That'd be great.

00:59:13.781 --> 00:59:16.705
&gt;&gt; So, no, I can't give you
an answer to that question.

00:59:16.706 --> 00:59:18.900
&gt;&gt; One take away is were very

00:59:18.901 --> 00:59:21.105
interested in
polymorphic encryption.

00:59:21.106 --> 00:59:25.245
So, that's probably a real key
collaboration opportunity.

00:59:25.246 --> 00:59:26.410
&gt;&gt; Yeah, okay.

00:59:26.411 --> 00:59:28.815
&gt;&gt; There are some

00:59:28.816 --> 00:59:31.095
awesome experts in
that in this building.

00:59:31.096 --> 00:59:33.660
&gt;&gt; Certainly, we'll have.
I don't have expertise.

00:59:33.661 --> 00:59:35.910
&gt;&gt; I'm not one either,
actually I'm a-.

00:59:35.911 --> 00:59:42.330
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] for me.

00:59:42.331 --> 00:59:45.730
&gt;&gt; So, you have more
about Spark on SGX?

00:59:46.970 --> 00:59:49.695
You're running Spark on SGX?

00:59:49.696 --> 00:59:52.540
&gt;&gt; Yes, that's right.
Spark set in on top of

00:59:52.541 --> 00:59:54.260
an SGX compliance Linux kind of

00:59:54.261 --> 00:59:56.085
library. So, if you Google-.

00:59:56.086 --> 00:59:58.995
&gt;&gt; You're running
Spark inside an SGX?

00:59:58.996 --> 01:00:02.250
&gt;&gt; Components of Spark. We
have to rewrite Spark or

01:00:02.251 --> 01:00:05.760
rather we have to SGX-fy
some specific parts of Spark.

01:00:05.761 --> 01:00:07.230
The project really was

01:00:07.231 --> 01:00:10.090
about identifying which
parts of Spark needed to

01:00:10.091 --> 01:00:13.170
be inside the SGX enclave

01:00:13.171 --> 01:00:15.600
and which parts
didn't need to be.

01:00:15.601 --> 01:00:17.620
So, we have to make
some subtle changes

01:00:17.621 --> 01:00:19.315
to the Spark code base.

01:00:19.316 --> 01:00:22.695
&gt;&gt; So, you're trying to be
training, re-eval or both?

01:00:22.696 --> 01:00:25.030
&gt;&gt; So, what we're
trying to do is have

01:00:25.031 --> 01:00:26.840
a Spark environment
so that users can

01:00:26.841 --> 01:00:28.790
do wherever they
want on top of it,

01:00:28.791 --> 01:00:31.485
just writing in
standard code in Spark SGX.

01:00:31.486 --> 01:00:34.645
The Spark SGX compliant version.

01:00:34.646 --> 01:00:36.680
The user's in variance
to the fact that it's

01:00:36.681 --> 01:00:38.375
got SGX sitting behind it.

01:00:38.376 --> 01:00:42.010
So, as a person that
doesn't want to have to

01:00:42.011 --> 01:00:45.890
worry about the kernel-ish
level operations,

01:00:45.891 --> 01:00:47.435
that one has to undertake

01:00:47.436 --> 01:00:49.465
to get SGX to
actually do anything.

01:00:49.466 --> 01:00:52.650
I can just sit there,
write my Scala code in

01:00:52.651 --> 01:00:55.910
Spark as I normally
would and be fairly

01:00:55.911 --> 01:00:58.810
confident that the SGX side

01:00:58.811 --> 01:01:00.800
of things will be
taken care of through

01:01:00.801 --> 01:01:02.485
this Linux library and

01:01:02.486 --> 01:01:06.470
the Spark modifications that
sits on top of the SGX LKL.

01:01:06.471 --> 01:01:08.620
&gt;&gt; Do you have estimates
on how the performance

01:01:08.621 --> 01:01:10.465
is related to the SGX version?

01:01:10.466 --> 01:01:11.580
&gt;&gt; From memory, I think there's

01:01:11.581 --> 01:01:20.340
a five times performance
penalty using SGX,

01:01:20.341 --> 01:01:23.100
but I believe Intel are
making some changes to

01:01:23.101 --> 01:01:26.545
the next formal release which
should help us with that.

01:01:26.546 --> 01:01:28.890
There's still issues
with SGX around such

01:01:28.891 --> 01:01:32.100
[inaudible] that
will always exist.

01:01:32.101 --> 01:01:37.105
But, I guess, Intel is claiming
that such [inaudible] don't exist.

01:01:37.106 --> 01:01:39.485
So, we're not fully secure
in the way that I described,

01:01:39.486 --> 01:01:41.400
but nothing ever is.

01:01:41.401 --> 01:01:42.120
&gt;&gt; You aren't secure as it is.

01:01:42.121 --> 01:01:46.470
&gt;&gt; Yeah, exactly. So, there's
a couple of papers, again,

01:01:46.471 --> 01:01:48.200
on our websites and

01:01:48.201 --> 01:01:50.090
all this software
now so open-sourced,

01:01:50.091 --> 01:01:53.070
so if you're interested
then grab it

01:01:53.071 --> 01:01:54.630
and spot the hooks

01:01:54.631 --> 01:01:56.545
and countries and
all the other good stuff.

01:01:56.546 --> 01:01:59.515
&gt;&gt; I think omission
of the MCMC on Spark?

01:01:59.516 --> 01:02:00.265
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:02:00.266 --> 01:02:05.110
&gt;&gt; So, what kind of
performance [inaudible].

01:02:05.111 --> 01:02:08.320
&gt;&gt; What we don't at
present, so we get

01:02:08.321 --> 01:02:09.820
a significant time in

01:02:09.821 --> 01:02:12.180
significant reduction in
computational performance,

01:02:12.181 --> 01:02:13.550
but at least we can then

01:02:13.551 --> 01:02:15.090
paralyze across
