01:02:15.091 --> 01:02:16.270
So, at the moments I

01:02:16.271 --> 01:02:17.620
would argue that one
can't do anything

01:02:17.621 --> 01:02:20.800
at all on Spark with MCMC.

01:02:20.801 --> 01:02:23.790
You can there's
a couple of attempts at

01:02:23.791 --> 01:02:26.999
this using kernel
based approximations

01:02:27.000 --> 01:02:28.530
plus you can use
kernel as samples

01:02:28.531 --> 01:02:29.895
and then combining those

01:02:29.896 --> 01:02:33.515
and I'll call it from my
perspective rather ad hoc way.

01:02:33.516 --> 01:02:34.970
So, one can do stuff but

01:02:34.971 --> 01:02:38.055
that there's no
asymptotic relationship.

01:02:38.056 --> 01:02:40.560
No guarantees that asymptotically

01:02:40.561 --> 01:02:42.895
those approximations
mean anything

01:02:42.896 --> 01:02:45.040
unless you combine all of
these different approximations.

01:02:45.041 --> 01:02:46.160
What we're trying to be

01:02:46.161 --> 01:02:47.640
statistically principled
when we combine

01:02:47.641 --> 01:02:50.790
these different local
approximations,

01:02:50.791 --> 01:02:52.590
but the penalty for
that at the moment is

01:02:52.591 --> 01:02:55.075
that costs you a lot
more computationally.

01:02:55.076 --> 01:02:57.290
So, the next challenge
is actually how do

01:02:57.291 --> 01:02:59.390
we improve the computational
performance of

01:02:59.391 --> 01:03:02.440
these quite horrific
rejection sampling

01:03:02.441 --> 01:03:05.055
related approaches
that we've proposed.

01:03:05.056 --> 01:03:08.320
That's what we'll be doing
next, but in the mean time,

01:03:08.321 --> 01:03:11.065
kind of open-source in
all these implementations.

01:03:11.066 --> 01:03:13.580
So that at least you know
if you have the time

01:03:13.581 --> 01:03:15.530
to wait and you care

01:03:15.531 --> 01:03:18.025
